Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/2] HID: opt-in radio diagnostics for Logitech C-U0019

This series proposes an experimental debugfs companion for the Logitech
C-U0019 receiver (046d:c542), without replacing hid-generic or usbhid.
The aim is to investigate reception problems, including possible USB3
interference, while preserving normal mouse input and driver bindings.

This is an architecture RFC, not a claim that this interface is ready for
production or that its RSSI interpretation has been established. Feedback
on whether this belongs in the kernel rather than a userspace tool is
particularly welcome.

The module observes the HID bus through a non-exclusive subsys_interface.
Reads default to disabled and require explicit opt-in. Active requests
are restricted to bcdDevice 0302, feature report 5 and five fixed four-byte
words. The response signature and observed DMA/RF layout are checked.
There is no arbitrary register access, firmware/OTP extraction, register
write, pairing command, background worker or automatic device binding.
SET_REPORT submits the C0 read request, followed by GET_REPORT; it is not
a firmware register-write operation. Undocumented access cannot be
claimed to be risk-free.

Four root-readable debugfs files separate identity, radio values, raw
registers and diagnostics. Radio/register opens share a one-second cache
and receive independent per-open snapshots. Suspended receivers are not
resumed, and protocol/transport failures stop further reads. The decoded
fields are deliberately named rssi_raw and frequency_mhz_candidate:
calibration, measurement age and correspondence to mouse signal strength
are unknown. PLL and RSSI are sequential reads, not an atomic channel
measurement. There is no dBm, link quality, packet loss or mouse-presence
claim.

Important unresolved questions:

* Is a non-exclusive HID bus observer appropriate for this diagnostic use?
* What arbitration should protect a vendor SET/GET pair from other hidraw
  feature clients? The private mutex does not provide that protection;
  concurrent vendor-feature clients are explicitly unsupported here.
* Are transport trylocks, active-only runtime PM references and the full
  debugfs proxy sufficient for all unbind/hot-unplug/system-PM paths?

Validation completed for this revision:

* GCC W=1 external-module build against 7.2.6-070206-generic headers.
* KUnit test source compiled as an object against those headers; the
  KUnit suite has NOT been executed (the test system lacks CONFIG_KUNIT).
* Exhaustive host tests of the exact protocol helpers on Linux and macOS.
* Nine unit tests of the separate capture/summary tools on both systems.
* Strict checkpatch source checks: no errors, warnings or checks.
* The two patches apply to the stated base and reproduce the commit tree.

Patch-mode checkpatch additionally emits its generic MAINTAINERS path
change advisory; existing HID and Logitech patterns cover the new files.
Signed-off-by is deliberately absent from this review draft: human review
and DCO certification are required before submission. The final RFC has
not been loaded on hardware. An earlier standalone prototype has limited
runtime test results, which are NOT claimed as validation of this series.

Physical unplug/replug, system suspend/resume, lockdep/KASAN stress,
allocation-failure tests, additional firmware units and controlled
USB3-near/far measurements remain outstanding. The capture tools and test
plan are kept outside the kernel patch series.

AI assistance was used for the module, protocol helpers, tests,
documentation and commit/cover-letter drafts. The task was to turn a
reverse-engineering prototype into a conservative optional companion,
keep the existing HID driver unchanged, restrict undocumented requests,
and make the remaining limitations and validation gaps explicit.
The commits include Assisted-by: LLM; the human submitter must review and
understand the complete contribution and add their own DCO sign-off.

Protocol background:
https://dev.to/endes/usb-hid-down-the-rabbit-hole-reverse-engineering-the-logitech-cu0019-usb-receiver-4d6a

